  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Sunday...\n\nAs an upper low exits the region this morning, a chance (20-40%)\nfor precipitation will linger over the east through the morning\nhours. Much of this precip will fall as a rain/snow mix, becoming\nall snow in the early morning hours near the Dakota border. There\nis a low chance (<20%) for patchy freezing drizzle over Fallon and\nCarter counties through sunrise. Currently, the highest chance \nfor more than a dusting of snow is near Baker, with a 20% chance \nfor more than 0.5\".\n\nAreas of fog and low clouds are likely (40-60% chance) this \nmorning, especially where precip has recently fallen and along \nriver valleys.\n\nA stronger upper trough is expected to aggressively push into the\nnorthern Rockies this afternoon, bringing a more widespread chance\n(30-60%) of precip for much of the region into Tuesday, along \nwith breezy winds of 25-45 mph (highest over the western \nfoothills) Tuesday. The speed of the track of this system has \ncontinued to increase, with the chance for precip ending for most \nby Tuesday afternoon. As a result, the chance for 0.25\" has \ndecreased. The Absaroka/Beartooth and Crazy mountains have the \nhighest chance at 70%, followed by northern Rosebud county at \n40%. Much of the rest of the plains will see a less than 20% \nchance. As for snow, a few flakes may still mix in over the \nplains, but overall the chance for accumulating snow is limited to\nthe mountains. Currently, there is a 40% chance for at least 2\" \nof snow above 7,000 ft over the western mountains, with locally \nhigher amounts over the west aspects of the highest peaks. For the\nnorthern Bighorns, the chance for snow continues to remain low, \nwith just a 35% chance of around 1\" accumulation on west slopes.\n\nFor the last half of the work week into the weekend, the pattern \nwill transition to southwest flow aloft over the region. This will\nallow occasional rain/snow showers over the western mountains and\nfoothills, but the lower elevations will likely remain mostly dry\nand breezy at times. \n\nHighs will remain near to slightly above seasonable through\nThursday and near seasonable through the weekend. Archer",
